{"input_text": "Question: I know that  $$\lim\limits_{  x\to + \infty}x\cdot \sin(\sqrt{x^{2}+3}-\sqrt{x^{2}+2})\\=\lim\limits_{  x\to + \infty}x\cdot \sin\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^{2}+3}+\sqrt{x^{2}+2}}\right).$$  If  $x \rightarrow + \infty$ , then  $\sin\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{x^{2}+3}+\sqrt{x^{2}+2}}\right)\rightarrow \sin0 $ . However I have also  $x$  before  $\sin x$  and I don't know how to calculate it. 

Answer: \begin{align}
   x \sin \left( \sqrt{x^2+3}-\sqrt{x^2+2}\right)
   &=x  \sin \left( \frac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+3}+\sqrt{x^2+2}}\right)\\
   &=\left(\dfrac{x}{\sqrt{x^2+3}+\sqrt{x^2+2}}\right)
     \dfrac{\sin \left( \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+3}+\sqrt{x^2+2}}\right)}
            {\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+3}+\sqrt{x^2+2}}\right)}\\
   &=\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{1+\frac{3}{x^2}}+\sqrt{1+\frac{2}{x^2}}}\right)
     \dfrac{\sin \left( \dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+3}+\sqrt{x^2+2}}\right)}
            {\left(\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+3}+\sqrt{x^2+2}}\right)}\\
   &\to \dfrac 12 \cdot 1  \ \text{as $x \to \infty$}\\
   &\to \dfrac 12 \ \text{as $x \to \infty$}
\end{align} 
